<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Move Around – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Dance! <b>Theme:</b> Move Around</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Show comprehension of storybook sequencing by pointing to pictures with the teacher's guidance.
reading_2: Match spoken words to their written form as they are heard and supported with pictures.
phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of storybook sequencing by pointing to pictures with teacher guidance (e.g., pointing to pictures of a character walking, then jumping, and finally dancing in a story).
Can match spoken action words to their written form when supported by pictures (e.g., matching the spoken word 'dance' to a picture of a person dancing and the word 'dance').
Can recognize letters of the alphabet in familiar words (e.g., 'J' in 'jump').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a quick "Movement Simon Says" activity using familiar action verbs from Lesson 1. "Simon says, 'dance'!" (students dance). "Simon says, 'jump'!" (students jump). "Simon says, 'take one step'!" (students take one step). This connects to the theme "Move Around" and activates vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces a large-format illustrated storybook titled "Let's Dance!" or a set of large sequential picture cards. The teacher models how to look at the pictures and track the words with a finger, pointing to each word as it is read aloud slowly and clearly. The teacher specifically models pointing to action words like 'dance' and 'jump' in the text while demonstrating the action.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a picture of someone dancing) "Is she walking or dancing?" (Expected: "Dancing!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to the word 'jump') "Does this word say 'jump' or 'spin'?" (Expected: "Jump!") (Teacher helps if needed, pointing back to the picture).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Dance!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large-format illustrated storybook with simple, repetitive action sentences (e.g., "First, we dance. Next, we jump. Then, we spin around.") using target vocabulary and sequential commands. Alternatively, large sequence cards with a single sentence and corresponding picture on each. The story introduces the sequence of a simple dance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading comprehension of simple pictorial sequences and matching spoken words to written words within a story context, supported by visual cues.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (Teacher-guided pointing):</b> The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to words. After each page/card, the teacher asks, "What are they doing?" and encourages students to point to the action in the picture (e.g., "Show me 'jump'!") or to the written word 'jump' if visible and previously modeled.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sequencing pictures:</b> The teacher holds up two picture cards from the story (e.g., "First, dance" and "Next, jump") and asks, "What happens first? Dance or jump?" Students point to the correct card. The teacher then lays out the pictures in the correct sequence as the story is reread.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Word-Picture Matching:</b> The teacher points to a word like 'dance' in the storybook and asks, "Where is the picture for 'dance'?" Students point to the corresponding picture.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice showing comprehension of sequential commands and matching spoken words to their written form with picture support. They will also recognize familiar letters.
                <br><b>Grammar:</b> Sequential commands with numbers ('First, take one step.', 'Next, take two steps.'), Simple directive phrases ('Move to the front.', 'Step to the side.'), Positional commands with prepositions ('Stand next to the chair.', 'Jump over the line.').
                <br><b>Vocabulary:</b> dance, jump, step, spin, turn, circle, line, one, two, in, out, around, next to, over.
                <br><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Recognizing letters 'J' in 'jump', 'S', 'T', 'U', 'V'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice – 'Match the Action' Activity:</b> The teacher prepares a set of large picture flashcards showing 'dance', 'jump', 'step', 'spin', and corresponding word cards. The teacher says a word (e.g., "dance"), and students point to the picture of someone dancing. Then, the teacher shows the word card "dance" and asks students to match it to the picture. This is repeated for all target words. Students also point to the initial letter of the word as the teacher calls it out (e.g., "Point to the 'D' in 'dance'").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled practice – 'Sequence & Read' Activity:</b> The teacher distributes small sets of sequential story picture cards (e.g., a character first standing next to a chair, then jumping over a line, then dancing around in a circle) to small groups. Beneath each picture, a simple sentence is written (e.g., "Stand next to the chair.", "Jump over the line.", "Dance around the circle."). The teacher reads the sentences aloud, and students point to the corresponding picture in their set. They then try to put the pictures in the correct 'First, Next, Then' order. The teacher highlights words with letters S, T, U, V.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction/Peer check – 'Find the Word' Game:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small board with 3-4 pictures (e.g., 'jump', 'dance', 'step') and separate word cards. One student picks a word card (e.g., 'jump') and says "Find 'jump'!" The other student points to the picture of 'jump' on their board and then places the word card next to it. They switch roles. The teacher circulates, offering support and prompting letter recognition (e.g., "What letter is this in 'jump'?" pointing to 'J').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Dance!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Students will participate in a "Dance Story Sequencing" activity. They will demonstrate their reading comprehension by collectively arranging large picture cards (with accompanying simple sentences) of a dance sequence and matching them to spoken commands, while also physically performing the actions. This requires them to "read" the sequence through pictures and match words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher places 4-5 large sequential picture cards from the "Let's Dance!" story (e.g., "First, take one step to the side.", "Next, take two steps.", "Then, spin around.", "Finally, jump up!") on the floor, out of order. Each card has the sentence clearly written below the picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher calls out a sequential command (e.g., "First, take one step to the side.") and asks students to find the matching picture card. Students collaboratively point to the correct card.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once the correct card is identified, one student is invited to place it in the correct sequence on a "story line" (e.g., on a long piece of string or tape on the floor).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">As each card is placed, the teacher rereads the sentence, and the whole class performs the action. For instance, "First, take one step to the side!" (students take one step). "Next, take two steps!" (students take two steps).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This continues until the entire dance sequence is correctly ordered and acted out. The teacher emphasizes matching the spoken command to the written word and picture. Throughout, the teacher also points out and reviews the target letters (S, T, U, V) within the words.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to point to sequential pictures, match spoken words to written words (with picture support), and recognize target letters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher shows students three simple action picture cards from the story (e.g., 'jump', 'dance', 'step') along with their corresponding word cards placed separately. The teacher then says one of the action words (e.g., "Point to 'jump'!") and students must point to the correct picture first, and then to the correct written word. The teacher also shows a flashcard with the letter 'J' and asks, "What sound does this letter make?" or "Find a word that starts with 'J'."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to point to the correct picture when a word is spoken (e.g., points to 'dance' picture when 'dance' is said).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to point to the correct written word when a word is spoken (with picture support).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of familiar letters (e.g., 'J' in 'jump', 'S', 'T', 'U', 'V') when prompted.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active group participation in sequencing activity, showing understanding of 'First, Next, Then'.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher holds up simple flashcards with a happy face and a sad face. "Did you understand the dance story today?" (Students show happy/sad face). "Can you find the word 'dance'?" (Students point to the word 'dance' on a word wall or card if they can).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite action word we read today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me the letter 'J' on a card?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific reading strategies (pointing, picture matching) worked best for demonstrating comprehension?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to successfully sequence the story pictures with guidance? What challenges did they face in matching spoken words to written words, or recognizing letters?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I further scaffold for phonemic awareness and letter recognition in the next lesson to build on their reading skills?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), students will orally describe the actions they read about today, using the vocabulary and sequential commands in simple sentences with peers, fostering more active language production.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>